1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.340
Look at the blue dot on your phone.

2
00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:08.680
It knows where you are.

3
00:00:08.680 --> 00:00:13.020
It can guide a driver through an unfamiliar city.

4
00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:17.360
It can help a ship approach a port.

5
00:00:17.360 --> 00:00:21.700
It can keep a clock in a network synchronised.

6
00:00:21.700 --> 00:00:39.750
But the dot raises a question most people never ask.

7
00:00:39.750 --> 00:00:57.800
Who owns the signal that makes it possible?

8
00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:04.667
India has said the concern plainly: civilian signals from foreign navigation systems can potentially be degraded or denied.

9
00:01:04.667 --> 00:01:11.533
That does not prove every dramatic story told about a past shutdown.

10
00:01:11.533 --> 00:01:18.400
It explains why an independent system matters.

11
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:56.700
So India developed NavIC, Navigation with Indian Constellation, a regional satellite navigation system.

12
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:02.040
The satellite broadcasts its position and a precise time reference.

13
00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:07.380
Your receiver compares that time with the arrival of the signal.

14
00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:12.720
The difference helps estimate distance.

15
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.060
But one distance is not enough.

16
00:02:18.060 --> 00:02:23.400
It tells you that you could be somewhere on a huge imaginary sphere around the satellite.

17
00:02:23.400 --> 00:02:27.520
Now add another satellite.

18
00:02:27.520 --> 00:02:31.640
Then another.

19
00:02:31.640 --> 00:02:35.760
Their distance measurements narrow the possible locations.

20
00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:39.880
The receiver's clock is not as precise as the satellites' clocks.

21
00:02:39.880 --> 00:02:44.000
So more measurements are needed, to solve for position and clock error together.

22
00:02:44.000 --> 00:03:09.000
Navigation is also a story about time.

23
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:34.550
NavIC gave India a regional navigation architecture under its own control.

24
00:03:34.550 --> 00:04:00.100
Its signals can also work alongside other global navigation systems in compatible receivers.

25
00:04:00.100 --> 00:04:04.660
Here is a fictional scenario.

26
00:04:04.660 --> 00:04:09.220
A fishing vessel receives signals from several navigation systems.

27
00:04:09.220 --> 00:04:13.780
One signal becomes unreliable.

28
00:04:13.780 --> 00:04:18.340
A compatible receiver can compare the remaining measurements, but only if enough usable signals are available.

29
00:04:18.340 --> 00:04:22.900
Resilience comes from both independence and redundancy.

30
00:04:22.900 --> 00:04:37.050
The blue dot looked simple.

31
00:04:37.050 --> 00:04:51.200
Behind it was a network of clocks, orbits and decisions.
